  "title": "Finding rules to describe numeric properties",
  "body": " Finding rules to describe numeric properties   The Locker Problem    There are 20 lockers in a hallway numbered 1 through 20. The lockers all start out closed. Twenty students numbered one through 20 are given the following instructions: Student 1 is instructed to go through and open every locker. Student 2 is instructed to start with locker 2 and close every second locker. Student 3 is instructed to start with locker 3 and change the status of every third locker, that is, open a closed locker and close an open locker. Student 4 is instructed to start with locker 4 and change the status of every fourth locker. Students 5 through 20 are given similar instructions; what lockers remain open after the 20th student has completed?      Suppose instead there were 100 lockers and 100 students; what lockers remain open?      For the scenario with 100 lockers and 100 students, which locker(s) changed the most? Which locker(s) changed the least?      Find a prime factorization for the number 84. How does the prime factorization relate to the locker problem?      Sigma Function   In mathematics we can use function notation to describe rules. For example, the divisor function counts the number of divisors of a given number. We will use the Greek letter sigma to denote this function. So, for example, we can write because the number of divisors of 6 is 4, that is 6 is divisible by the numbers 1, 2, 3, and 6.     Evaluate       Evaluate      The prime factorization of a number can be used to help us determine the value of our sigma function. Consider the prime factorization of the number 72 which is If we wanted to list all of the factors of 72, we recognize that for any factor it can have either 0, 1, 2, or 3 factors of 2 and 0, 1, or 2 factors of 3. Therefore we see the factors of 72 are:                        Listing out in this manner we see that given 4 different options for the factor 2 and 3 different options for the factor 3, there are different factors for 72, and thus      Given that the prime factorization of 432 is determine the value of       Find a number, such that       Sum of consecutive numbers   In this example, we investigate which numbers can be written as the sum of consecutive positive integers. We can write as the sum of two positive consecutive integers, We can write as the sum of three positive consecutive integers, . We can write as the sum of 4 consecutive integers, . What we are interested in investigating is whether every positive integer can be written as the sum of consecutive positive integers.     We begin our investigation with the sum of two positive integers. Begin by filling in the table of values and then describe any patterns you notice about the sum of two integers.                                We continue our investigation with the sum of three positive integers. Begin by filling in the table of values and then describe any patterns you notice about the sum of three integers.                                Fill in the table by expressing each number (when possible) as the sum of consecutive integers. Some of these values will require 4 or more consecutive numbers to be expressed. After you've filled in all values that can be expressed as the sum of consecutive positive integers, look for a pattern to describe the numbers that can't be expressed.                                                                Without doing the calculations predict which of the following numbers can be written as the sum of 2 consecutive numbers, 3 consecutive numbers, 4 consecutive numbers, 5 or more consecutive numbers, or cannot be written as the sum of consecutive numbers.    45      57      60      64      100        Find a set of consecutive numbers that will add up to the following numbers:    151      204      112          "
