  "title": "Order of operations",
  "body": " Order of operations   The Order of Operations is an agreed upon order in which operations must be completed when simplifying a numeric or an algebraic expression. When presented as a list of rules, the Order of Operations can seem arbitrary. However, there are mathematical reasons underlying the order in which operations are completed. In general we preform more complicated operations first and then work down in terms of complexity.  Our simplest operation is addition which is undone by subtraction. Next, we consider multiplication which we consider repeated additions. For example, can be thought of as adding 4 together 3 times or . Multiplication is undone with division, so they are treated as the same level of complexity. Next, we consider exponents which can be considered repeated multiplications. For example, can be thought as multiplying 3 by itself 4 times, that is Finally, if we want to alter the order we use a grouping symbol, generally parentheses or fraction bars in this course. So our order of operation follows:    G  Grouping symbols, including parentheses and fraction bars    E  Exponents and Radicals (left to right)    M  Multiplication and Division (left to right)    A  Addition and Subtraction (left to right)       Consider the following expressions. Evaluate each of these following the prescribed order of operations.                          Three Threes   Consider the Three Threes Clock Face shown below. Verify that each number on the clock face is represented correctly using three threes. Think about how you are using the Order of Operations to check each expression.    A clock with each digit represented using three threes with appropriate order of operations      Verify that each expression is correct. Show your work.      Find other expressions for some of the numbers 1 through 12 using three threes. Show your work.      Four Fours   Use the number 4 exactly four times to write expressions equivalent to the numbers from 0 through 20. You may use any of the mathematical symbols below. A few examples are provided.   Arithmetic operations:     Parentheses: ( )    Decimal points or percents:  , , 44%    Exponentiation: ^ (to the power of),      Square root:      Factorial: ! (     Concatenation: 44, 444    Repeating decimal:         Complete the table. Find at least one expression using 4 fours for each number. Use the Order of Operations; include grouping symbols as needed. You may check your responses using a graphing calculator. Some examples are provided, for the numbers whose solutions are shown in the table, find at least one more expression that works.        Number  Four Fours Equation  Number  Four Fours Equation    0   12     1     13     2   14     3   15     4   16     5   17     6   18     7     19     8   20     9   24       10   30     11   100         Which of your solutions are not dependent on the number 4? For example is also true when each 4 is replaced with 2, 6, or 9. Highlight these solutions in your table.      Using math rules    Without using a calculator determine which is greater, or ?      Rewrite the expression using order of operations to show how much greater the larger value is.     "
